WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.335
Server applications make legitimate
outbound requests for webhooks,

2
00:00:04.415 --> 00:00:08.251
link previews, image retrieval, document
imports, feeds, callbacks,

3
00:00:08.331 --> 00:00:10.632
remote validation, and third-party APIs.

4
00:00:10.712 --> 00:00:13.269
These features cross a network trust
boundary.

5
00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:16.673
The server often has routes, service
identity, proxy access,

6
00:00:16.753 --> 00:00:19.565
or credentials that the requesting client
does not.

7
00:00:19.645 --> 00:00:22.458
When a client can choose where that server
connects,

8
00:00:22.538 --> 00:00:27.076
an ordinary integration feature can become
a general-purpose network capability.

9
00:00:27.156 --> 00:00:31.759
The deliberately unsafe statement
fetch(req.body.url) captures the design flaw in

10
00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:32.350
one line.

11
00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.284
Fetch is not inherently insecure,

12
00:00:34.364 --> 00:00:37.688
and TypeScript does not change the runtime
trust of the value.

13
00:00:37.768 --> 00:00:41.987
A type assertion such as req.body.url as
string says what the program expects;

14
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:45.263
it does not prove that the input is a
permitted destination.

15
00:00:45.343 --> 00:00:49.306
Successful URL parsing would prove only
that the value has URL structure.

16
00:00:49.386 --> 00:00:52.199
Neither step authorizes a server-side
connection.

17
00:00:52.279 --> 00:00:55.283
The impact is broader than returning a
secret response.

18
00:00:55.363 --> 00:00:58.239
A server-side request may reach an
internal service,

19
00:00:58.319 --> 00:01:02.474
trigger a state-changing endpoint, consume
bandwidth or connection slots,

20
00:01:02.554 --> 00:01:05.751
or interact with a network location that
trusts the server.

21
00:01:05.831 --> 00:01:09.027
The response can be hidden and the request
can still matter.

22
00:01:09.107 --> 00:01:13.134
Authentication of the caller does not
solve this boundary problem either.

23
00:01:13.214 --> 00:01:16.793
A logged-in user remains an untrusted
source of destination data.

24
00:01:16.873 --> 00:01:20.709
A defensible outbound operation starts
with an application decision,

25
00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:22.131
not a user-supplied URL.

26
00:01:22.211 --> 00:01:26.110
Define the operation and legitimate
destination set; parse the target;

27
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.642
enforce protocol, hostname, and port
policy; resolve the name;

28
00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:34.452
classify every candidate address; control
redirects; and constrain the network path.

29
00:01:34.532 --> 00:01:38.751
Limit methods, credentials, time, and
response volume as separate controls.

30
00:01:38.831 --> 00:01:42.794
The central question is not whether the
submitted string looks like a URL.

31
00:01:42.874 --> 00:01:47.285
It is whether the application explicitly
permits the destination the server will

32
00:01:47.365 --> 00:01:48.260
actually reach.
